course: dictionary
lesson: 2913
title: Draft: a first try at writing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A draft is an early version of a piece of writing, a drawing or a plan. It is not finished yet, and it is meant to be improved. «A draft is a version of your writing that you plan to improve.» So when you write a first try, you are writing a draft.
explain: Drafts are normal. Writers plan, draft, edit and redraft. The first draft is often messy, and that is fine. Then you check your spelling, punctuation and word choice, and make the writing better.
explain: To draft is a verb: it means to write or draw the first version of something. You can draft a story, a poster or an email. «Keeping notes and drafts shows that your work is your own.» Keep your messy notes so people can see how your work grew.
fact: A draft is an early version that is not final yet.
fact: A first draft, a rough draft and a final draft are different stages of the same piece of writing.
fact: To draft something means to write or draw the first version of it.
fact: In email and apps, unsent messages are often saved in «Drafts».
fact: In the story «Austin's Butterfly», a boy drew a butterfly six times, and kind, specific feedback from classmates made each draft better.
exercise: «A draft is a version of your writing that you plan to improve.» What is a draft? | A rule for spelling | A finished book on a shelf | A cold wind through a window | *An early version that is not finished yet
exercise: Mr Okafor says, «Your first draft can be messy.» What should you do next? | Hand it in straight away without changes | *Improve spelling, punctuation and word choice | Throw it away and never write again | Print it and stop
exercise: Which word means to write or draw the first version of something? | Publish | Hide | Final | *Draft
test: Why do writers keep notes and drafts? | So they can forget their ideas | To show that the work is their own | To make the paper heavier | *To show that the work is their own, and to see how it grew
test: After the messy first draft, what comes next? | Sending it off unchanged | *Editing and redrafting to improve it | Starting a new topic | Ignoring it completely
exam: Which sentence uses draft as a noun, meaning an early version? | I will draft the letter tonight | They drafted three emails before lunch | *Her first draft had lots of crossings-out | Please draft a plan for the trip
